  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Hemiodus argenteus is a shoaling hemiodontid characin from Venezuela, Guyana, Suriname and Brazil, where it inhabits floodplain rivers and lakes. Like its congeners it can be very skittish when first introduced but usually settles over time, especially in a large group. Very active and growing to around 235 mm SL, it needs a large tank with a well-fitting cover, as it can leap surprising distances.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Venezuela, Guyana, Suriname, Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Inhabits floodplain rivers and lakes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Very active and needs a tank measuring at least 120 × 45 × 45 cm (48 × 18 × 18 in) - about 243 litres (64 US gal).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>A biotope setup would consist, very simply, of a layer of a couple of inches of inert sand as substrate, with perhaps some driftwood branches and twisted roots scattered around. Some floating vegetation will help to make it less skittish, though, and it's equally at home in a fully planted setup. In either case, the water should be very well-oxygenated, and make sure the tank has a well-fitting cover as it can leap surprising distances. It's sensitive to poor water conditions, so tank maintenance must be stringent.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 23–27 °C (73–81 °F)</li><li>pH: 6.0–7.0</li><li>Hardness: 3–12 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Unfussy, and will accept most live, frozen and dried foods. Try to make sure it receives some greenstuffs in its diet, as it's reported to feed quite heavily on plant matter in nature.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Very peaceful but it can intimidate much smaller or more quiet species with its activity and size. It makes an ideal shoaling species for the larger Amazon biotope setup, where it can be combined with similarly-sized characins, cichlids such as Geophagus, Satanoperca and Heros, catfish and Loricariids. It's a shoaling species by nature and should always be kept in a group.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Unknown. Mature females are likely to be rounder-bellied than males.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Not known to have been bred in the hobby. It's probably an egg-scatterer.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/hemiodus-argenteus",
      "html": "<p>Hemiodus species can be a very skittish when first introduced to the aquarium, but usually settle down over time, especially if a large number are being kept together. All of them look superficially similar, but can be distinguished by differences in patterning. None are particularly common in the hobby.</p>"
    }
  ],
